  "articleBody": "Upgrading your master bath with marble adds enduring value, but natural stone behaves differently than standard ceramic. When planning marble, homeowners often focus entirely on appearance and overlook moisture dynamics. \n\nA common pitfall is treating marble as a waterproof barrier. Natural stone and grout are porous materials; water will migrate through them, which means a dedicated, continuous waterproofing membrane behind the stone is mandatory. Another frequent issue is choosing high-gloss polished marble for shower pans. Polished stone becomes slick when wet, making a honed finish or smaller mosaic tile a safer practical choice for footing. Homeowners also run into trouble by skipping layout planning. Cutting natural veins around niches without dry-fitting leads to disjointed patterns and wasted material. Lastly, using standard tile adhesives can lead to chemical staining, as improper mortars bleed into pale marble.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options:\nBalance stone finishes against your household habits. Honed marble softens reflections, conceals water spots, and provides traction, while polished stone highlights deep veining on accent walls.\n\nWhat to Do Next:\nWhen you want flawless stone fabrication and proper moisture protection handled under one roof, contact Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 for your Aurora or Denver area bathroom renovation.",
